## Safe polymorphic-behavior simulator

```text
BASE_MARKER = "DEFENSIVE-LAB-CANARY-42"


function sha256(data):
    return Hash(data)                 // Used only for fingerprints


function xor_stream(data, seed):
    result = empty byte array
    counter = 0

    while length(result) < length(data):
        block = sha256(seed || integer_to_bytes(counter, 4 bytes))
        append block to result
        counter = counter + 1

    return bytewise_xor(
        first length(data) bytes of data,
        first length(data) bytes of result
    )


function make_generation(generation_number):
    // Deterministic: the same generation always produces the same result.
    seed = sha256("defensive-lab/" || generation_number)

    plaintext = UTF8(BASE_MARKER)
    transformed = xor_stream(plaintext, seed)

    return {
        generation: generation_number,
        seed: seed,
        transformed_bytes: transformed,
        sha256: sha256(transformed)
    }


function recover_marker(generation):
    recovered = xor_stream(
        generation.transformed_bytes,
        generation.seed
    )

    assert UTF8_STRING(recovered) == BASE_MARKER
    return recovered


function run_simulation(number_of_generations):
    generations = []

    for i from 1 to number_of_generations:
        generation = make_generation(i)

        // This only verifies the inert marker. Nothing is executed.
        assert recover_marker(generation) == BASE_MARKER

        append generation to generations

    return generations
```

Running `run_simulation(5)` would produce five different transformed byte sequences and five different SHA-256 fingerprints, while every generation decodes to the same inert marker. That models the defensive-relevant property: **constant behavior with changing representation**.

